    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "so wait, i'm confused. why did you mention trolls and goblins? im just trying to learn how the hashing works. and can you go back to the rainbow table part? how exactly does the salt mess up the precomputed table part?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the fantasy creatures. Let's focus on the hashing and rainbow tables.\n\nSo, when you hash a password, you create a fixed-length string of characters that represents the password. This is like a unique identifier for the password. However, if you use the same password multiple times, you'll get the same hash, which is like finding the same treasure chest in the forest - it's not very secure.\n\nRainbow tables are precomputed tables of hashes for common passwords. An attacker can use these tables to look up the original password for a given hash. This is like having a map of the forest that shows where all the treasure chests are located.\n\nNow, when you add a salt to the password before hashing it, you're essentially creating a new, unique hash for each password. This is like hiding the treasure chest in a different location each time you bury it. Even if the attacker has a map of the forest, they won't be able to find the treasure chest because it's in a different location.\n\nThink of it like this: if you have a password \"password123\" and you hash it without a salt, the resulting hash might be \"abc123def\". If you use the same password again",
